WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:06.080
Jeremy Foley, Director and Co-Writer: THE
FACELESS MAN is a man who is down-and-out.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:07.770
He’s depressed.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:13.929
He wakes up one morning and his face has been
transformed into a mirror.

00:00:13.929 --> 00:00:18.720
This story was adapted from a short story
that my father wrote in a reaction to The

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:19.720
Great Recession.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:21.600
And I think it certainly was how he was feeling

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.720
Journalist voiceover: “This situation is
that many Americans have lost their jobs and

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:25.890
they’ve lost their homes.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:29.810
Frankly, a lot of the ones I spoke to feel
like they’ve lost their identity.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:32.119
They don’t see a recovery.

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:35.410
Instead they must find a new purpose in today’s
world.”

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:36.410
“Gimme a break."

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:42.120
Jeremy Foley: I remember thinking that idea
(a man searching for his lost identity) would

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.670
resonate with a lot of people.

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:50.070
Sara O’Reilly, Producer: This story had
a lot of humor but also some serious themes

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:53.320
and all of that we wanted to preserve when
we were adapting it.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:58.010
It’s like the spirit of this whimsical adventure
that the faceless man goes on.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:04.010
Bill O’Leary, “The Faceless Man”: I
personally love juxtaposition of comedy and

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:08.159
tragedy that are right on top of each other
and something I really enjoyed performing.

00:01:08.159 --> 00:01:09.820
“Nothing to see here.”

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:11.820
Even within this fantastic story.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:14.790
I really wanted to bring the guy’s pain
and effort.

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:15.790
“I’m sorry.

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:17.200
I just woke up like this.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.960
Please don’t arrest me.”

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:23.360
Sara O’Reilly: When we first started figuring
out how we were going to make this film (how

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:26.930
were we going to make a man with a mirror
for a face), we decided we were going to make

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:27.930
it practically.

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:31.700
Will Myers, Director of Photography: I wasn’t
quite sure what the ins-and-outs were going

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:37.450
to be and so it required a lot testing just
to make sure of the reflection aspect and

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:41.330
whether or not it was going to get the desired
effect, if it was going to show the camera

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:42.330
and those kinds of things.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:48.020
“It is weird because you do not change in
size no matter how much close I move the camera.”

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:52.080
Yes it was a huge pain in the a$$ but it looks
so much better and I’m glad we did it this

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.080
way.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.609
Jeremy Foley: The next step was about the
fabrication of the mask.

00:01:55.609 --> 00:02:01.679
Sara O’Reilly: So we hired a company called
Rogue Planet Laboratories which is Ian Goodwin’s

00:02:01.679 --> 00:02:06.159
shop and worked with him to really develop
a mask.

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:11.850
We did a full life cast of Billy’s face
where they pour all of the goop all over his

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:18.460
head and make a mold which we have like a
perfect bust of Billy now in our living room.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:23.000
And then using that mold he then sculpted
that shape of what the mask what was going

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.310
to be with clay so we kind of had an idea
of what it was going to look like.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:30.990
Then he molded it with silicone and silicone
kind of looked like skin but it’s really

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:35.940
pale, so once they apply that to Billy’s
face, he then had to paint it.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:40.250
So he airbrush paints it and blends it perfectly
with Billy’s skin.

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:43.340
So for three hours every morning Billy was
in the chair and make-up.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:46.200
Bill O’Leary: Just the work they did was
just mind blowing.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:49.830
And for me, I’ve been doing this work a
long time and I’ve never had an experience

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:50.830
before.

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:54.800
Sara O’Reilly: It was important to be able
to have the mirror on and off because we wanted

00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:01.720
Billy to be able to breathe and drink water
and eat and eat cylindric foods or things

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:02.720
on forks.

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.250
I can’t imagine doing what he did, it was
very impressive.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:10.970
Bill O’Leary: It’s a lot like 14 hours
of him just taking his hands and pressing

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:18.780
them on your face around your eyes in 106
degree….yeah like that!

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:19.780
For 14 hours..

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:21.690
Ian Goodwin: Except it’s not as much fun.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:23.640
Bill O’Leary: But not as much fun!

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:24.640
“Action!”

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:28.220
Jeremy Foley: A lot of different disciplines,
a lot of different filmmaking techniques went

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:30.930
into making this short film.

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:36.190
Will Myers: There are a couple of Easter egg
digital shots in the film but 98% of it is

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:37.190
all practical.

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:40.270
Jeremy Foley: While the practical aspects
of it work well as the mirror in terms of

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:44.810
a mirror, performance-wise and lighting-wise
and everything else that came along with it

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:47.430
became exponentially harder.

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.000
“Down…down…okay…now to the right.”

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:53.520
Jeremy Foley: So we had this fun thing on
the set called “The Mirror Game.”

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:57.860
“Tilt up your chin…yeah…closer…closer.

00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:03.040
Head down…tilt down…and tilt it left…tilt
left…head up.”

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:08.980
With the mirror we working working always
with the tiniest degree of give or take.

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:13.340
If Billy had turned his head just a half an
inch to the left or just a half an inch to

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:16.870
the right, it would have revealed ten people
standing in that corner.

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:21.819
Bill O’Leary: I was having to just over
and over and over I mean crazed and then I

00:04:21.819 --> 00:04:25.800
come and see a kid and they’d go “No,
you are off by a billionth of an inch.”

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.110
And then I’d go “Okay, I’ve got to go
see a kid.”

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:29.110
And then “No!

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:31.030
Now you are off by a quarter of an inch.”

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:32.030
Aaarrgggh!!!

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:33.030
“Go out a bit.”

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:36.070
I will never have difficult sharing a footmark
again in my life.

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:40.750
Those are easy, this was tricky.

